        "titulo": "How Russian really sounds",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: in Russian the STRESSED syllable is strong and the UNSTRESSED ones weaken: this is REDUCTION. STEP 2: unstressed «О» sounds like «А»: «молоко» [ma-la-KOH], «хорошо» [kha-ra-SHOH], «вода» [va-DAH]. STEP 3: unstressed «Е» and «Я» sound like «И»: «сестра» [sis-TRAH], «язык» [yi-ZIK], «весна» [vyis-NAH]. STEP 4: the «Г» in «его, сегодня, чего» sounds like «В»: «его» [yi-VOH], «сегодня» [si-VOD-nya]. STEP 5: «ЧТО» sounds [SHTOH] and «КОНЕЧНО» sounds [ka-NYESH-na]. STEP 6: «ЗДРАВСТВУЙТЕ» has a silent «В»: [ZDRAH-stvooy-tyeh]. STEP 7: voiced consonants at the end of a word become unvoiced: «хлеб» [khlyep], «год» [goht]. STEP 8: the written text does NOT show the reduction: that is why listening a lot is essential.",
        "comparacao": "English also reduces unstressed vowels (the first \"a\" in \"about\" and the last \"a\" in \"sofa\" are a weak \"uh\"), but Russian reduces more regularly: an unstressed «о» becomes a short \"ah\".",
